 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and memory complexity:** Time complexity scales with dataset size and threshold distribution, as high-density regions require expensive polygon intersection calculations while low-density regions use efficient splatting [S1]. Memory complexity requires storage for grid data, slicing planes, and intermediate polygonal representations [S1].
-**Measured performance:** Not given in the sources [S1].
-**Scaling:** Supports parallel processing for interactive visualization of large datasets [S1].
-**Strengths and limitations:** Strengths include reducing aliasing in sparse regions while maintaining geometric accuracy in dense regions [S1]. Limitations involve the need for careful tuning of threshold and plane spacing, and potential visual discontinuities at the threshold boundary [S1].
-**When to use and when not:** Use for datasets with significant density variations where both accuracy and performance are critical [S1]. Do not use for uniformly dense datasets where pure slicing or splatting is more efficient [G].
+**Time Complexity:** The complexity is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of cells, but with a significantly smaller constant factor for the splatting portion. The thresholding step is linear. The polygonal rendering is proportional to the number of high-value cells, while splatting is proportional to the number of low-value cells.
+**Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the cell lists.
+**Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+**Strengths:** Reduces aliasing for small or low-value structures; improves performance by simplifying the geometry of low-value cells; provides exact boundaries for high-value structures.
+**Limitations:** Requires two rendering passes or a hybrid compositing strategy; potential for visual discontinuities at the threshold boundary.
+**When to use:** Large datasets with a wide range of scalar values where both fine details (low value) and clear boundaries (high value) are important.
+**When not to use:** When uniform geometric accuracy is required across all values, or when the threshold cannot be meaningfully defined.
 
 ## Background
-Proposed in the context of volume rendering for curvilinear and unstructured grids [S1]. Builds on Yagel et al.'s polygonal slicing and Zwicker et al.'s anti-aliased ellipsoidal splats [S1]. Developed to address interactive visualization challenges for large, heterogeneous datasets, with parallel processing implementations for scalability [S1].
+The method was proposed by Silva et al. in 2003 as part of a broader framework for volume rendering of curvilinear and unstructured grids [S1]. It builds on the idea of slicing cells into polygons (Yagel et al. [4]) and combining it with anti-aliased ellipsoidal splatting (Zwicker et al. [5]) to handle large datasets efficiently [S1].
 
